  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT SAT JUL 25 2026\n\nThe broad upper-level pattern continues to pull the front out of\nthe region, leaving rain chances on the more scattered side\ntoday. Here, showers and storms will mainly be driven by another\nweak impulse this morning, with activity generally decreasing in\ncoverage as the day progresses. By Monday, we're still expecting\nthe pattern to shift, with the ridge building to the east from\nthe Plains. Now, this is where things get interesting. While we\nstill anticipate the ridge to build in and temperatures to climb,\na perturbation in the flow between the trough to our east, and\nridge to our west may allow for an MCS to work in from the north\nMonday. Guidance first started suggesting this the other day, and\nsince then the signal has only grown stronger. While confidence\nin the MCS itself continues to grow, confidence in the exact size\nand strength of the MCS currently remains low. As such, I have\nonly introduced low rain chances for Monday, mainly limiting them\nto along and north of I-20.\n\nRain chances decrease again on Tuesday, which is shaping up to be\nour hottest day of the week. Under the ridge, afternoon highs\nshould climb into the mid to upper-90s. Thankfully, the ridge\nappears to become slightly suppressed into Wednesday, as the\ntrough begins to build back in over the East Coast, with a deep\nlow pressure forming over New England. Afternoon highs should\nslowly drop into next weekend, as this trough allows for some\nslightly cooler temperatures to filter in from the north. While\nit's still going to be hot, temperatures by next Friday should\ngenerally range in the upper-80s and low-90s for most of the\narea. This trough could also allow for additional MCS's to move\nthrough the region beginning on Wednesday, resulting in more\nwidespread rainfall. Confidence in these additional MCS's\ncurrently remains low, with only limited rain chances in the\nforecast for the rest of the upcoming workweek.\n\n/44/",
